diff --git a/CHANGELOG.md b/CHANGELOG.md
index b9f3f8b..c671d1e 100644
--- a/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -40,7 +40,7 @@ See `CLAUDE.md` §6 for the entry style guide.
 - `standalones/passwords/Get-WeakPassword.ps1`: reads standard input when given `-` as a path, or when stdin is redirected (a shell pipe) and no other source is specified — so `cat file | pwsh -File Get-WeakPassword.ps1` works from any shell, matching `password-scrape.sh`'s `-`/pipe convention. `pwsh -File` does not bind redirected stdin to the `ValueFromPipeline` parameter the way an in-session pipeline (`Get-Content x | .\Get-WeakPassword.ps1`) does; both now work. Guarded on `IsInputRedirected` so an interactive run never blocks waiting on the console, and suppressed when `-Path`/`-History` supplied a source.
 
 ### Fixed
-- `standalones/windows/Invoke-HostPosture.ps1`: enumerated registry policies no longer fall through to PASS on an out-of-domain value — `LDAPServerIntegrity`/`LdapEnforceChannelBinding` outside 0..2 and `LmCompatibilityLevel` outside 0..5 now grade ERR (before, `LdapEnforceChannelBinding=3` graded AD-LDAP-SIGNING PASS).
+- `standalones/windows/Invoke-HostPosture.ps1`: registry policies can no longer fall through to PASS on an unexpected value — every enumerated/boolean policy is read through one validated reader (`Get-RegPolicy`) against its documented value set, and any other value (out of range, negative, non-numeric; also `AutoAdminLogon` and a non-version `PowerShellVersion`) grades ERR. Before, e.g. `LdapEnforceChannelBinding=3`, `LDAPClientIntegrity=3`, `ConsentPromptBehaviorAdmin=6`, LAPS `BackupDirectory=4` or `UseLogonCredential=2` graded PASS.
 - `standalones/linux/host-posture.sh`: sshd `Include` patterns are split with `read -ra` instead of an unquoted expansion, so under `ARANUM_POSTURE_ROOT` a glob is never expanded against the live filesystem before it is re-rooted (hermetic runs could read the real host's `sshd_config.d`).
 - `network/report.py`: specific product rules (Tomcat, Jenkins, Spark, Solr, SSDP/UPnP, GitLab, Hadoop, …) are no longer shadowed by the generic `CRITICAL`/`EXPOSED`/`UNAUTH` fallbacks — the four generics are composed **last** at every rule-assembly site (`_compose_rules`), so a specific rule always wins first-match and keeps its own severity and KB `rule_id`.
 - `network/report.py`: a dispatcher-marked critical (`[!!]` / `CRITICAL`) can never be downgraded by a lower-severity rule or an operator `--severity-rules` custom rule (unconditional critical floor), and distinct criticals receive distinct line-derived KB keys that preserve the URL path (so `.../admin` and `.../debug` do not collapse to one key).
